            <ArticleTitle>The intertester reliability of the Scapular Assistance Test.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>653-60</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Test-retest reliability study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine interrater reliability of the modified scapular assistance test (SAT). The modified SAT is designed to assess the contribution of scapular motion to shoulder pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Abnormal scapular motion has been implicated in different shoulder disorders. However, there is a lack of clinical evaluation tools to assess the scapular component of shoulder dysfunction.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS AND MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Forty-six subjects who were referred to physical therapy for treatment of various shoulder pathologies were recruited for this study. The modified SAT was performed on each participant by 2 different examiners. Percent agreement and kappa coefficient were utilized to determine interrater reliability of the modified SAT.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The kappa coefficient and percent agreement were .53 and 77%, respectively, when the test was performed in the scapular plane, and .62 and 91%, respectively, when the test was performed in the sagittal plane.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The modified SAT possesses acceptable interrater reliability for clinical use.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
